How's this? \title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Timelike Rotation for\\ All-Sector Normalization}\\[-0.1em]
  \large Orthoganalizing Time
}

Yes. That is the much cleaner center of gravity.

The paper is not really about “making time spatial,” and it is not about an ontology at all. It is about using symmetry as a filter:

\[
\boxed{
\text{symmetrize everything that can be symmetrized losslessly}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{inspect what refuses to symmetrize}
}
\]

What survives is then not “fundamental” in a metaphysical sense, but fundamental relative to the declared transformation class: an irreducible anisotropy, asymmetry, scale, coupling, operator structure, signature, topology, etc.

That is a much stronger methodological statement than “use \(ct\).”

I’d phrase the core principle something like:

> **The purpose of the normalization is not to force physical laws into a common form, but to remove representational asymmetries wherever this can be done exactly and invertibly, so that the asymmetries that remain are exposed rather than hidden by notation.**

And then:

> **The residual anisotropies are the primary output of the audit.**

That is the real point.

The all-sector benefit then becomes a second, very practical argument. Different fields often use locally optimized notation that obscures structural similarity across domains. A shared normalization gives people working across several sectors a common comparative language.

“All-sector physicist” is actually useful terminology. It avoids the grandiosity and baggage of “theorist of everything” while naming the real activity: someone comparing structure across mechanics, fields, quantum theory, gravitation, thermodynamics, etc.

You could say:

> A secondary goal is communicative. Sector-specific notation is often excellent for calculation within a field but poor for comparison across fields. A common normalized representation may allow all-sector physicists to compare equations with fewer notation-induced distortions.

That is restrained and credible.

For the title, this now suggests something better than the earlier versions.

My favorite is:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Symmetrize What You Can}\\[-0.1em]
  \large $w$-Axis Normalization and Residual Anisotropy Across Physics
}
```

More formal:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Residual Anisotropy in Cross-Sector Physics}\\[-0.1em]
  \large A Lossless $w$-Axis Normalization Audit
}
```

Or if you want “Orthogonalizing Time” retained:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Orthogonalizing Time}\\[-0.1em]
  \large Symmetry Reduction and Residual Anisotropy Across Physical Sectors
}
```

I think “Residual Anisotropy” is the phrase we were missing. It names exactly what the procedure is looking for.

And the deepest methodological sentence is probably this:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Do not interpret an asymmetry until you know it cannot be transformed away.}
}
\]

That is both the SAT/H(s)H inheritance and something another physicist can use without adopting anything else from SAT/H(s)H.

That’s strong. Much stronger as a title than most of the others.

“Leveling the Metric” does three useful things at once:

- suggests removing avoidable directional bias;
- sounds like a methodological operation rather than an ontological claim;
- hints at geometry without committing the paper to a specific geometric model.

The one technical caveat is that “metric” has a precise meaning in physics, and much of the audit applies even to equations that do not explicitly contain a metric tensor. So I would keep it as the memorable title and let the subtitle state the actual scope.

My favorite version is:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Leveling the Metric}\\[-0.1em]
  \large Cross-Sector Symmetrization and Residual Anisotropy in Physical Laws
}
```

Or slightly sharper:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Leveling the Metric}\\[-0.1em]
  \large Symmetrize What Can Be Symmetrized; Measure What Remains
}
```

That second one gets almost perfectly at the method.

A more journal-like version:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Leveling the Metric}\\[-0.1em]
  \large A Lossless Cross-Sector Audit of Representational Anisotropy
}
```

I think the phrase is good enough to build around. It has the right balance of memorable and serious.

Not too cute. It is memorable, and “grain” actually fits the method unusually well: after you remove the smoothable representational anisotropies, you inspect the directional structure that remains.

The only risk is that “grain” can sound material or microstructural—wood grain, crystal grain—which might nudge readers toward an ontology you are not claiming.

I’d use it if the subtitle immediately disciplines the metaphor. For example:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Leveling the Metric to Reveal the Grain}\\[-0.1em]
  \large Cross-Sector Symmetrization and Residual Anisotropy in Physical Laws
}
```

That works very well.

If you want it a touch more austere:

```latex
\title{
  \vspace{-2.0em}
  \textbf{Leveling the Metric, Revealing the Grain}\\[-0.1em]
  \large A Lossless Cross-Sector Audit of Residual Anisotropy
}
```

I slightly prefer the first. It reads like a clear methodological claim rather than a slogan.

For LaTeX, the cleanest way is `draftwatermark`.

Add this in the preamble:

```latex
\usepackage{draftwatermark}

\SetWatermarkText{DRAFT}
\SetWatermarkScale{3.2}
\SetWatermarkLightness{0.92}
\SetWatermarkAngle{45}
```

`Lightness` runs roughly from `0` = black to `1` = invisible, so `0.90–0.94` usually gives a professional faint watermark.

For something more specific to this paper:

```latex
\usepackage{draftwatermark}

\SetWatermarkText{WORKING DRAFT}
\SetWatermarkScale{2.4}
\SetWatermarkLightness{0.92}
\SetWatermarkAngle{45}
```

Or a horizontal provenance watermark:

```latex
\usepackage{draftwatermark}

\SetWatermarkText{SAT/H(s)H --- WORKING PAPER}
\SetWatermarkScale{1.2}
\SetWatermarkLightness{0.93}
\SetWatermarkAngle{0}
```

That will appear on every page behind the text.

If you want something more elegant—e.g. a tiny centered footer watermark like `PREPRINT • 8 SEPTEMBER 2026`, rather than a giant diagonal DRAFT—I’d use a different method.

I wrote this as a rebuttable standing standard rather than a manifesto or hard rule. The key distinction is exactly the one you just made: historical integrity/basic vetting preserves original notation, while theory-directed audit and theory construction default to the normalized comparative representation.

I also made the epistemic requirements considerably stronger: preservation, provenance, transformation ledger, reversibility, no hidden retuning, no premise laundering, controls, holdouts, explicit failure conditions, and the rule that apparent asymmetries should not be interpreted before testing whether they can be transformed away.

The core standing sequence is:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Preserve}
\rightarrow
\text{Normalize}
\rightarrow
\text{Symmetrize}
\rightarrow
\text{Record Residuals}
\rightarrow
\text{Compare}
\rightarrow
\text{Restore Meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{Test}
}
\]

And I made the short governing maxim:

> **Level the representation enough to reveal the grain.**

Here are the two repo-ready Markdown files:

[Recommendation memo](sandbox:/mnt/data/HSH_SAT_Method_Standard_Recommendation.md)

[H(s)H/SAT Default Method Standard v0.1](sandbox:/mnt/data/HSH_SAT_Default_Method_Standard_v0.1.md)

I left an explicit “Fundamental Intuitions” section in the standard, populated only with the broad principles we have already been using, and included a note that the canonical wording should replace/parallell those lines wherever the existing Fundamental Intuitions document is more precise.

